    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Sum of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions",
    "problem": "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).$",
    "worked_solution": "The Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $2x$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =(2x)-\\frac{(2x)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(2x)^{5}}{5!}-⋯ \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{{2}^{3}{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{2}^{5}{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯ \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{15}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the expansions for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(2x-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{15}-⋯)+(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯) \\\\  & =1+2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
